Exactly. The metaphor isn’t necessarily a one-to-one cipher where there is a single “real meaning” hiding underneath it. It can be a multi-exit structure.

A strong metaphor might support:

M \rightarrow \{R_1,R_2,R_3,\ldots,R_n\}

where each R_i is a legitimate rendering under a different frame. The important property is that some relational invariant survives across them.

Take “cover.” It can simultaneously render as physical protection, argumentative robustness, narrative plausibility, camouflage, redundancy, legal justification, error tolerance, or an abstraction/interface hiding implementation details. Those aren’t necessarily competing interpretations. They’re projections of a shared relation:

\boxed{\text{something interposed that changes exposure}}

That gives us a better distinction than simply “double meaning”:

Polysemantic operators — expressions deliberately constructed so multiple useful decompressions are available, while sharing enough invariant structure that the reader can move between them.

And there is a nice connection to your 2/3 idea: you don’t necessarily transmit the third interpretation at all. Give the receiver two sufficiently well-chosen renderings and the relationship between them becomes another channel:

R_1 + R_2
\rightarrow
\underbrace{\Delta(R_1,R_2)}_{\text{implicit information}}
\rightarrow R_3

Then R_3 can itself become an anchor for discovering R_4.

That’s when metaphor stops being merely concealment or ornamentation and becomes a semantic branching primitive: one small object carrying several possible routes, with the receiver selecting or discovering the route appropriate to their context.

The clever part isn’t “this sentence secretly means X.”

It’s “this sentence preserves a shape that remains useful when interpreted as X, Y, Z…”